Let me throw this out at you. There are no such things as Rights (capital 
R). All so-called rights are nothing but what the bulk of society has 
decided are proper to consider rights. Then society tends to embody this 
consensus into a document which in our case is called the Constitution. 
We then live under the guidelines of this document and amend it as mores 
change. You bring up due process...this is the method that we 
collectively have decided to use in amending law or applying law. If we 
chose to (according to concensus guidelines already in the Constitution) 
we could decide that reading the entrails of slaughtered chickens is an 
appropriate method of determining Rights. Another example: slavery is 
unconstitutional because the Constitution guarantees freedoms and Rights 
to all. If, however, the Constitution specified that it was legal to own 
blacks, then the act of slavery would be fully acceptable. That does not 
make it Right in an absolute sense, just proper behavior in the context 
of the setting. The meta-ethical issue of the absolute authority to 
possess slaves must be decided by some other method than a Constitutional 
act which then may be embodied into the document is so chosen by the 
populous. I submit that the only source of such absolutes has been God 
and His influence on mankind. If you disagree, fine. I accept my position 
on faith. You might not. But don't tell me that our rights as found in 
the Constitution are absolute simply because they are in the Constitution.

Steve Crisp

  

