>do so and stick to it. Following that moment, it would take extremely 
>strong evidence to the contrary to budge him. What liberals can't stand 
>and call closed-mindedness is when conservatives refuse to accept their 
>drivel as truth and alter positions.

A comment here: 'open discussion' is not the same as 'open-minded'.
I agree that Robertson would probably be happy to engage in an open
discussion of these issues; the question is whether such discussion
would be meaningful.  The reason those like Robertson are referred
to as 'closed-minded' is that their positions are founded on 
absolute faith in premises for which they can give no reasons
other than that they believe them.  If one has an absolute and
unshakeable faith in some position, then discussion and evidence
('strong' or otherwise) become laregely irrelevant.  

One thing that underlies 'liberalism' (under almost any definition)
is that no human being can guarantee his or her possession of
absolute truth, and thus must be willing to consider the possibility
that someone else's position may be closer to the truth than his or
her own.  This is what underlies the support of open-mindedness and
open discussion.  If one has absolute faith in one's own position
and is unwilling to consider even the possibility that one is in
error, then one is closed-minded and open discussion has no point.


>One of the defining self-referential characteristics of modern liberals 
>is the embracing of moral relativism as the superstructure of their 
>philosophy. They define themselves.

The above description may be considered 'relativism' by some, but
this is not necessarily the case.  'Liberals' may well believe
that absolutes exist; what they doubt is that any person has a 
